# 8. BACKGROUND DOCUMENT INDEXING PIPELINE

Implement document ingestion using BullMQ and Redis.

The user-facing upload API must not wait for a large document to finish indexing.

### Processing pipeline

1. Authenticate the uploader.
2. Validate organization membership and upload permission.
3. Validate file size, file type, and file contents.
4. Upload the file to private object storage.
5. Create the document record in MongoDB.
6. Create an indexing job.
7. Extract text in a background worker.
8. Extract page and section metadata.
9. Normalize and clean extracted text.
10. Split text into overlapping semantic chunks.
11. Generate embeddings in batches.
12. Store chunks and embedding vectors in MongoDB.
13. Update document indexing status.
14. Record processing duration and token usage where available.
15. Notify the user when processing completes or fails.

### Chunking requirements
- Configurable chunk size and overlap
- Token-aware splitting where possible
- Preserve page numbers
- Preserve headings and section metadata
- Preserve source offsets where supported
- Avoid empty chunks
- Avoid duplicate chunks
- Support documents containing many pages

Each chunk should store:
- Chunk ID
- Organization ID
- Document ID
- Page number or page range
- Chunk index
- Text content
- Embedding vector
- Section title
- Relevant metadata
- Created timestamp

### MongoDB storage design for chunks

Use a dedicated `documentChunks` collection.

Store embeddings as BSON numeric arrays compatible with the selected Atlas Vector Search index configuration.

Create the required vector index with:
- Embedding field
- Vector dimensions matching the selected embedding model
- Similarity metric
- Organization ID filter field
- Document ID filter field
- Workspace ID and other supported filter fields

Use a separate, versioned index or controlled migration when the embedding model or dimensions change.

Do not store embeddings as unstructured strings.

Do not retrieve all chunks into application memory to calculate similarity manually.

### Reliability
- Retry transient failures with exponential backoff
- Set maximum retry counts
- Support failed-job inspection
- Handle provider rate limits
- Implement concurrency limits
- Process large documents in batches
- Make jobs idempotent
- Prevent duplicate indexing
- Support worker restarts
- Add timeouts
- Add dead-letter or terminal-failure handling
- Record structured error information without leaking sensitive document content

Do not store fake embeddings or mark documents as indexed before successful completion.

